  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/error-codes"
  eyebrow="Dryer Error Codes · Brand Reference Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Dryer Error Codes, Explained by Brand"
  subtitle="LG, Samsung, Whirlpool, and GE — what the codes mean, which point to a clogged vent, which you can clear yourself, and when it's a tech job."
  heroImageTitle="Dryer Error Codes by Brand"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Start here</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Most dryer codes mean one thing: restricted airflow.</h2>

    <p><strong>Across every brand, the most common dryer error code points to restricted airflow — a clogged lint filter or a blocked exhaust vent. LG calls it the Flow Sense d80/d90/d95 set, Whirlpool and Maytag show AF, and Samsung's heating codes (HC/hE) usually trace back to the same cause. The fix is almost always cleaning the filter and the vent, not replacing a part. Two things to know up front: LG's CL is not an error (it's the child lock — hold the button to clear it), and a power-interruption code (PF) clears with a simple unplug-and-restart. Sensor, heater, and control codes that survive a full vent cleaning are the ones that need a tech. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why airflow codes matter:</strong> a restricted dryer vent isn't just why your clothes take three cycles — trapped lint and heat are a leading cause of household dryer fires. That's why an airflow code is worth clearing the same day, not next month.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 LG -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · LG dryers</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG codes — mostly airflow, sensor, and door.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>d80 / d90 / d95 — Flow Sense airflow restriction</h3>
      <p>LG's airflow warnings at rising severity (roughly 80/90/95% blockage). Clean the filter and vent and they clear themselves. Full walkthrough on our <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-d80-error/">LG d80/d90/d95</a> guide.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>tE1 / tE2 — thermistor (temp sensor)</h3>
      <p>The temperature sensor is reading out of range. Rule out a clogged vent first (it fakes a high reading); if the vent is clear and it returns, the thermistor needs a tech.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>dE — door · FO — filter out · PF — power failure</h3>
      <p>dE means the door isn't fully closed (reseat the filter, clear the door). FO means the lint filter isn't seated. PF means the cycle was interrupted by a power blip — hold Start to resume.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>PS — power/wiring · CL — child lock (not an error)</h3>
      <p>PS points to a 240V power-cord or terminal-block wiring problem (a tech/electrician job). CL is simply the child lock — hold the Child Lock button ~3 seconds to clear it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SAMSUNG -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Samsung dryers</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung groups codes by family.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating family — HC / hE</h3>
      <p>The dryer detected an overheat. The most common cause is a clogged vent, so clean the exhaust first. If it persists on a clear vent, the heater or a thermostat needs a tech.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Temperature family — tS / tO / tE</h3>
      <p>A thermistor reading out of range. Same order: clean the lint screen and vent, then if it returns it's a sensor replacement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door family — dC / dE / dF</h3>
      <p>The door is open or not latching. Check for an obstruction and that the door clicks shut; a persistent code means the door switch or latch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Power family — 9E1 / 9C1 / FC</h3>
      <p>These flag an abnormal voltage supply or power frequency. Confirm the dryer is on a proper dedicated circuit and the cord is installed correctly; if it is, it's an electrician or tech call.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 WHIRLPOOL / MAYTAG -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Whirlpool &amp; Maytag dryers</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool and Maytag share a short, blunt code set.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>AF — restricted airflow</h3>
      <p>Whirlpool's airflow code: clogged lint screen, blocked vent, or kinked duct. Clean the path end to end and touch any key to dismiss it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>PF — power failure · L2 — low/no voltage</h3>
      <p>PF means the cycle was interrupted by an outage — hold Start to resume. L2 means the heater isn't getting proper line voltage; check the breaker and fuses (a 240V dryer trips one leg and still tumbles without heat), and if those are fine it's an electrician/tech call.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>F# E# — service fault · E1 / E2 (model-specific)</h3>
      <p>A generic F-then-E code means the control found a fault that needs service. On some models an E1 or E2 points to an exhaust thermistor reading out of range — that's an ohm-test-and-replace tech job. Note: "Sensing" and "Wet" on the display are normal cycle-status messages, not error codes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 GE — HONEST FRAMING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · GE dryers</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE dryers usually don't show a code at all.</h2>

    <p>This is the honest answer, and it saves you chasing a chart that doesn't apply: most GE dryers are diagnosed by symptom, not by a digital error code — they simply don't display one. Some newer GE front-load (GFD-series) models can show a fault code, but GE's code formats vary by model and generation and there's no single consistent published list, so a generic "GE dryer code chart" online will often be wrong for your exact machine. The reliable move on a GE is to check your model's own manual for what it displays.</p>

    <p>The good news is the diagnostic logic doesn't change with the badge. Whatever a dryer shows (or doesn't), the order is the same: airflow and power first, then the sensor, heater, and control. Our techs diagnose GE dryers by behavior — how it heats, tumbles, and cycles — which is exactly how GE intends them to be serviced.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · What you can safely do</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the common codes yourself.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the vent, end to end.</strong> This clears the airflow codes on every brand — LG d80/d90/d95, Whirlpool/Maytag AF, and most Samsung heating codes. Pull and wash the filter, clear the flexible duct behind the dryer, and check the exterior vent flap opens freely.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-cycle the dryer.</strong> Unplug it for a minute and restart. This clears PF power-interruption codes and transient glitches.</li>
      <li><strong>Release the child lock (LG CL).</strong> Hold the Child Lock button ~3 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door.</strong> For door codes, confirm nothing blocks the door and it clicks fully shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Note if the code returns.</strong> Cleared and gone = airflow or a glitch. Comes back on a clean vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter and vent cleaning, power cycling, and the child lock are safe homeowner work and fix the large majority of dryer codes. Do not open the dryer cabinet to test the thermistor, heating element, or control board, and never run a gas dryer with the gas line disturbed. A code that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A code that outlasts a clean vent means a failed part.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and a thermistor, heating, door-switch, or power/wiring code keeps returning, the part has to be tested and replaced. Our techs carry the common LG, Samsung, and Whirlpool thermistors, thermostats, and door switches, so most dryer-code calls are a one-visit fix. We verify the vent ourselves first — there's no point replacing a sensor that was reading a real blockage.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/dryer-repair/">dryer repair</a> hub, the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-d80-error/">LG d80/d90/d95</a> guide, and if your dryer runs but clothes stay wet, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a>. A chronically restricted vent is worth a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-dryer-repair/">LG</a>, <a href="/brands/samsung-dryer-repair/">Samsung</a>, <a href="/brands/whirlpool-dryer-repair/">Whirlpool</a>, and <a href="/brands/ge-dryer-repair/">GE</a> dryer repair. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">08 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Dryer error codes: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 09 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Dryer code back after cleaning the vent? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

